// Go workspace tying together the Ball Go implementation modules so they
// resolve against the local tree during development, at the same version their
// go.mod files publish (see the replace block at the bottom).
//
//   go/runtime  — dependency-free runtime value model (package ballrt)
//   go/shared   — generated proto bindings (package ballv1)
//   go/compiler — Ball → Go compiler
//   go/encoder  — Go → Ball encoder
//   go/engine   — self-hosted engine
//   go/cli      — the `ball` CLI (run/compile/encode/check)
go 1.23

use (
	./cli
	./compiler
	./encoder
	./engine
	./runtime
	./shared
)

// The five modules that other modules `require` are pinned back to this tree at
// the version their go.mod files name (v0.1.0 — the first published Go module
// tag set, see go/cli/AGENTS.md). A workspace `use` block alone is NOT enough:
// Go still loads the module graph, so a `require` on a version that is not yet
// on the module proxy fails with "unknown revision go/<m>/v0.1.0" even for a
// module that is itself in the workspace (verified with go 1.25). Keeping the
// pin here — instead of a `replace ... => ../<dep>` inside each module's own
// go.mod — is what makes the published go.mod files resolvable off
// proxy.golang.org: `go install` refuses any module whose go.mod carries
// replace directives, and go.work is never published.
replace (
	github.com/ball-lang/ball/go/compiler v0.1.0 => ./compiler
	github.com/ball-lang/ball/go/encoder v0.1.0 => ./encoder
	github.com/ball-lang/ball/go/engine v0.1.0 => ./engine
	github.com/ball-lang/ball/go/runtime v0.1.0 => ./runtime
	github.com/ball-lang/ball/go/shared v0.1.0 => ./shared
)
